---
// src/pages/guides/palm-care.astro
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const businessName = import.meta.env['PUBLIC_BUSINESS_NAME'] || 'Palm & Agave Nursery';
---

<Layout
  title="Understanding Palm Height, Clear Trunk, and Mature Size — Plant Guides | Palm & Agave Nursery"
  description="How palm measurements work — overall height, clear trunk height, crown spread, and mature size. What to expect as palms grow and how to select palms for Tampa Bay landscapes."
>
  <Header cartCount={0} />

  <div class="guide-page">
    <article class="guide-article container">
      <header class="guide-header">
        <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <ol class="breadcrumb-list">
            <li><a href="/" class="breadcrumb-link">Home</a></li>
            <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
            <li><a href="/guides" class="breadcrumb-link">Guides</a></li>
            <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
            <li class="breadcrumb-current" aria-current="page">Palm Care</li>
          </ol>
        </nav>
        <p class="guide-eyebrow">Palm Care</p>
        <h1 class="guide-title">Understanding palm height, clear trunk, and mature size</h1>
        <p class="guide-meta">Palms are sold and described differently from most landscape plants. This guide explains how palm measurements work so you can compare plants accurately and choose the right palm for your space.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="guide-content">
        <p class="guide-intro">
          Unlike a shrub or small tree, a palm is not measured the same way from purchase to maturity. You may see overall height, clear-trunk height, container size, and mature height all listed separately. They each mean something different, and they are not interchangeable.
        </p>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Overall height</h2>
          <p>
            Overall height is the full height of the palm from the base of the trunk to the top of the crown. For a container-grown palm, this is the height of the plant as it is now, roots and all, in its container.
          </p>
          <p>
            Overall height is the most useful single measure for comparing palms at a glance, but it does not tell you everything. A palm with a long clear trunk and a small crown may look very different from a palm with the same overall height but a shorter trunk and fuller crown.
          </p>
          <p>
            When you see a palm listed as 8 ft overall, that means the plant stands about 8 ft tall right now. It does not mean the trunk will stay at 8 ft forever, and it does not tell you how much of that height is trunk versus foliage.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Clear trunk height</h2>
          <p>
            Clear trunk height is the height of the trunk from the base to the bottom of the living crown — the point where the lowest fronds emerge. This is the portion of the trunk that is visually clean, without fronds or boots attached.
          </p>
          <p>
            Clear trunk height matters because it shapes the look of the palm. A palm with a tall clear trunk reads as more tree-like and architectural. A palm with a short clear trunk may look more like a large, rounded head of foliage sitting closer to the ground.
          </p>
          <p>
            In landscaping, clear trunk height is often what people mean when they talk about a palm having a "trunk" or being "tree-like." It is also the measurement most relevant to clearance under the crown — for walkways, driveways, and sight lines.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not confuse overall height with clear-trunk height. A palm that is 10 ft overall may have only 4 ft of clear trunk, with the remaining 6 ft made up by the crown. Always check both numbers if the space matters.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Container size versus field-grown</h2>
          <p>
            Container-grown palms are raised in pots. They are easier to transport, easier to plant, and easier to schedule. Their root system is contained, and their size is generally more predictable at the time of purchase.
          </p>
          <p>
            Field-grown palms are grown in the ground and dug for sale. They can be larger, more established, and sometimes more visually mature at the time of purchase. They may also have a heavier root ball and require more care in handling and planting.
          </p>
          <p>
            The catalog marks field-grown palms where applicable. If a palm is field-grown, expect a larger specimen and plan for appropriate unloading and handling. Confirm access for delivery vehicles, and if needed, arrange for equipment to move large palms on site.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Mature height and spread</h2>
          <p>
            Mature height is the approximate height a palm can reach under good conditions over time. Mature spread is the approximate width of the crown at maturity.
          </p>
          <p>
            These numbers are planning references, not promises. Actual size depends on species, climate, soil, water, sunlight, wind, and time. A palm may reach its mature height faster in ideal conditions and slower in a less ideal site.
          </p>
          <p>
            Use mature size to think about the future of the planting. A palm that looks fine at 8 ft may become a 30-ft specimen in the right conditions. If you are planting near a building, power line, or driveway, give the palm the room it will eventually need.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Growth rate</h2>
          <p>
            Palms vary widely in growth rate. Some species put on height relatively quickly, while others are slow and deliberate. Growth rate affects how soon a palm will fill in a space, but it does not necessarily tell you how large the palm will ultimately become.
          </p>
          <p>
            Fast-growing palms may reach a usable landscape size sooner and may need more frequent maintenance. Slow-growing palms may take longer to establish a presence but can be longer-lived and more stable in form. Neither is universally better — it depends on the goal.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want a palm that will make a statement quickly, look at growth rate alongside overall height and clear-trunk height. If you want a palm that will stay proportionate in a smaller space for longer, a slower species may be a better fit.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Cold tolerance</h2>
          <p>
            Cold tolerance is the approximate lowest temperature a palm can handle. As with agaves, this is a guide, not a guarantee. Actual survival depends on duration, humidity, wind, plant health, and how established the palm is.
          </p>
          <p>
            In Hillsborough County, cold-tolerant palms are the safer choice for exposed sites and for buyers who want to minimize winter risk. Less cold-tolerant palms can work in warmer microclimates or protected locations, but they carry more risk during cold snaps.
          </p>
          <p>
            When selecting a palm for a specific site, think about exposure, proximity to the coast, and whether the site tends to be warmer or cooler than the surrounding area. A palm that is borderline for the region may do well in a protected courtyard and poorly in an open field.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Sun exposure</h2>
          <p>
            Most landscape palms prefer full sun or bright partial shade. Some species tolerate more shade than others, and a few are better adapted to filtered light. The catalog lists sun exposure where known.
          </p>
          <p>
            Palms grown in too much shade may produce weaker, thinner fronds and may stretch or lean toward light. Palms in appropriate sun generally produce stronger crowns and more reliable form. If a palm is described as tolerating partial shade, it usually means filtered light or morning sun, not deep shade.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Trunk characteristics</h2>
          <p>
            Palm trunks vary in diameter, texture, color, and shape. Some are smooth and grey, some are ringed, some are covered in fiber, and some are noticeably stout or slender. Trunk characteristics are part of the palm's visual identity and can matter in a design.
          </p>
          <p>
            A palm with a stout, smooth trunk can read as substantial and formal. A palm with a slender, fiber-covered trunk can read as more natural or tropical. Trunk characteristics are listed where they are useful for identification and design.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Crown and fronds</h2>
          <p>
            The crown is the head of the palm — the fronds and the growing point at the top of the trunk. Crown shape, frond length, and frond density affect the palm's overall appearance and the amount of shade or screening it provides.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some palms have a full, rounded crown. Others have a more open, graceful crown with arching fronds. Some have stiff, upright fronds. These differences matter for the visual character of the landscape and for practical concerns like clearance and wind exposure.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Planting</h2>
          <p>
            Plant palms at the same depth they were growing in the container. For field-grown palms, follow the grower's guidance and avoid planting too deep. The root ball should be settled properly, and the palm should be staked if needed for stability.
          </p>
          <p>
            General planting guidance:
          </p>
          <ol class="guide-list guide-list--ordered">
            <li>Choose a location with appropriate sun exposure and enough room for the palm's future size.</li>
            <li>Dig a hole wide enough for the root ball and no deeper than necessary.</li>
            <li>Place the palm so the base of the trunk is at the appropriate level. Do not bury the trunk deeper than it was growing.</li>
            <li>Backfill and firm the soil around the root ball.</li>
            <li>Water thoroughly after planting.</li>
            <li>Stake the palm if it needs stability, especially for larger or field-grown specimens.</li>
            <li>Keep the planting area clear of heavy mulch against the trunk.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            For large specimen palms, confirm unloading and handling in advance. Some palms require equipment to move safely on site.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Long-term care</h2>
          <p>
            Palms need ongoing attention, but the most important care is often simple: water appropriately, avoid damaging the trunk and crown, and remove dead fronds when needed. Do not over-prune or over-fertilize.
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li>Water newly planted palms regularly until established, then adjust based on rainfall and soil drainage.</li>
            <li>Avoid burying the trunk or piling soil and mulch against it.</li>
            <li>Remove dead or thoroughly brown fronds as needed, but do not over-prune. A palm needs its healthy foliage.</li>
            <li>Watch for signs of stress, pests, and nutrient issues. Yellowing, browning, or slowed growth can indicate a problem.</li>
            <li>Protect cold-sensitive palms during cold snaps if needed.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If a palm is growing in the right place with the right conditions, it generally needs less intervention than many people expect. The goal is to support the palm, not to constantly manage it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section guide-section--cta">
          <p class="guide-cta-text">
            Ready to choose a palm? Browse our palm catalog, filter by height, trunk, cold tolerance, and sun exposure, and contact us if you need help matching a palm to a specific site or project.
          </p>
          <div class="guide-cta-links">
            <a href="/shop/palms" class="btn btn-primary">Shop Palm Trees</a>
            <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-outline">Ask a Question</a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .guide-page { padding: var(--space-10) 0 var(--space-16); }
  .guide-article { max-width: 760px; }

  .breadcrumb { margin-bottom: var(--space-8); padding-bottom: var(--space-5); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .breadcrumb-list { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-2); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-muted); }
  .breadcrumb-link { color: var(--color-text-secondary); text-decoration: none; transition: color var(--transition-fast); }
  .breadcrumb-link:hover, .breadcrumb-link:focus-visible { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .breadcrumb-current { color: var(--color-text-primary); font-weight: var(--font-medium); }

  .guide-eyebrow { font-size: var(--text-xs); font-weight: var(--font-semibold); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.12em; color: var(--color-sage); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .guide-title { font-size: clamp(var(--text-2xl), 4vw, var(--text-4xl)); color: var(--color-green-deep); line-height: 1.15; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .guide-meta { font-size: var(--text-base); color: var(--color-text-muted); max-width: 580px; line-height: 1.6; }

  .guide-content { margin-top: var(--space-10); }
  .guide-intro { font-size: var(--text-lg); line-height: 1.75; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }

  .guide-section { margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }
  .guide-section-heading { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--text-xl); color: var(--color-green-deep); margin-bottom: var(--space-5); padding-bottom: var(--space-3); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .guide-section p { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.75; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }

  .guide-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .guide-list li { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-text-secondary); padding-left: var(--space-5); position: relative; }
  .guide-list li::before { content: '—'; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--color-sage); }

  .guide-list--ordered { counter-reset: guide-ordered; list-style: none; }
  .guide-list--ordered li { counter-increment: guide-ordered; }
  .guide-list--ordered li::before { content: counter(guide-ordered) '.'; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--color-green-deep); font-weight: var(--font-semibold); }

  .guide-section--cta { padding: var(--space-8); background-color: rgba(41, 79, 59, 0.04); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); }
  .guide-cta-text { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-5); }
  .guide-cta-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-3); }
</style>
